# Python Standards

General Python conventions. For FastAPI router, schema, and API-layer code, see **`fastapi-standards.mdc`**. For secrets and environment file handling, see **`security-standards.mdc`** (always applies).

## Project structure

- Separate directories for source code, tests, docs, and config
- Place source under `src/<package_name>/` and tests under `./tests/`
- Modular design: distinct files/modules for models, services, controllers, and utilities
- Add `__init__.py` when creating packages under `./tests/` or `./src/<package_name>/`

## Style & formatting

- Line length: 100 (ruff)
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return types, including explicit return types (`None` where appropriate)
- Prefer `X | None` over `Optional[X]`
- Use `Self` for class methods that return the instance
- Use `ruff` for linting and formatting
- Use `mypy` with `ignore_missing_imports = true`
- Use `strict=True` in `zip()` when lengths must match

## Dependencies & tooling

- Manage dependencies with **uv** and virtual environments
- Implement CI/CD with GitHub Actions or GitLab CI

## Configuration

- Manage configuration via environment variables

## Documentation

- Google-style docstrings on all public functions and classes (PEP 257 compliant)
- Include `Args` and `Returns` sections
- Update existing docstrings when modifying code
- Keep any existing comments in a file

## Testing

- Use **pytest** only — not `unittest`
- Place all tests under `./tests/`
- Test classes/functions prefixed with `Test`
- All tests must have type annotations and docstrings
- Use `@pytest.mark.asyncio` for async tests
- Mock external services (GCS, WandB, etc.) in tests

When tests use pytest fixtures under `TYPE_CHECKING`:

```python
from typing import TYPE_CHECKING

if TYPE_CHECKING:
    from _pytest.capture import CaptureFixture
    from _pytest.fixtures import FixtureRequest
    from _pytest.logging import LogCaptureFixture
    from _pytest.monkeypatch import MonkeyPatch
    from pytest_mock.plugin import MockerFixture
```

## Validation & retries

- Prefer **Pydantic** for data validation and schema definitions
- Prefer **tenacity** for retries on external calls (HTTP APIs, cloud SDKs, etc.)
- Be explicit about which exception types are retriable — whitelist transient errors (timeouts, connection errors, rate limits, 5xx), never catch broad `Exception`
- Never use retries to mask code bugs — `TypeError`, `ValueError`, `KeyError`, and similar logic errors must fail immediately
- Set sensible retry limits (attempt count, backoff, max wait) and log retry attempts

## Error handling

- No defensive programming — be deliberate, intentional, and explicit about which errors and exceptions to catch
- Avoid bare `except:` — use specific exception types
- Log errors with `logging.exception()` for full tracebacks, including context capture
- Clean up resources (temp files, sessions, blobs) on errors
- Re-raise with `raise e` (not bare `raise`) after cleanup

## Logging

PII and secrets in logs are covered by **`security-standards.mdc`**. Apply these Python/cloud practices otherwise.

### Cloud & containers

- Log to **stdout/stderr**, not local files — container platforms (Cloud Run, ECS, K8s, Lambda) capture stdout and ship to CloudWatch, Cloud Logging, etc.
- Use **structured logging** (JSON) via `structlog` or `python-json-logger` — queryable fields beat prose in cloud log explorers
- Configure logging once at app startup; set level via environment variable (`LOG_LEVEL`)

### Log levels

- `DEBUG` — local troubleshooting only; off in production by default
- `INFO` — normal business events (request handled, job completed)
- `WARNING` — recoverable oddities (retry succeeded, deprecated path used)
- `ERROR` — operation failed; needs attention
- `CRITICAL` — service unavailable or data loss risk

### Context & correlation

- Propagate a **`request_id`** (or trace ID) through the request lifecycle — middleware, service calls, background tasks
- Include structured context: `service`, `environment`, `request_id`, `duration_ms`, `error_type` — not bare `"Something went wrong"`
- Use `contextvars` to bind request-scoped fields so loggers pick them up automatically

### Errors

- Log exceptions **once at the catch boundary** with `logging.exception()` — do not re-log the same error at every re-raise layer
- Return safe messages to clients; keep diagnostic detail in logs only

### Retries

- Log retries at `WARNING`; log final failure at `ERROR` — not every attempt at `ERROR`

### Cost & noise

- Do not log full payloads on every successful request
- Do not log inside tight loops or hot paths
- Avoid duplicate logs across layers (router, service, and repository logging the same event)

### Observability

- **Logs** — discrete events and forensics
- **Metrics** — rates, latencies, queue depth (CloudWatch, Prometheus) — do not use logs as a metrics system
- **Traces** — request path across services (OpenTelemetry, X-Ray, Cloud Trace) where applicable

### Background jobs & serverless

- Include `job_id`, `attempt`, and queue/task name in worker logs
- In Lambda/Cloud Functions: configure logging outside the handler; log start, end, and duration inside the handler

